Fillets: the corner at the end of a blend that rolls on an edge up to its
vertex and whose other contact overflowed onto a face beyond the face of
the spine's edge (mitcad#121): e.g. the notch of 0052 with a tab under its
straight side and round corner; the rounding ends at the plate's bottom,
inside the faces of the straight side and the corner, which run on below.

- ChFi3d_Builder::PerformOneCorner (OnSame end): the end of the side
  rolling on the obstacle edge is that edge's vertex; its arc is the
  obstacle edge, whose other face (the one the blend consumes) is not the
  face at the end. The point now gets the edge at that vertex between the
  face the blend touches and a face holding the corner's vertex, so the
  face at the end is that face (ChFi3d_ObstacleVertexOnEndFace). Before,
  the consumed face was taken for the face at the end.
- In that case the prolonged edge at the corner's vertex is looked for
  first among the edges not of the consumed face.
- Where the face of the far contact (Fop) does not hold the corner's
  vertex (the contact overflowed onto it, beyond the face of the spine's
  edge Fprol0), the face at the end is prolonged to the cork along Fprol0
  from the vertex to the edge between Fprol0 and Fop, which is cut where
  it crosses the face at the end, and along Fop from there to the end of
  the contact (ChFi3d_CapAcrossFaceBeyond). Before, the prolongation was
  looked for along Fop from the vertex, which does not lie on it, and the
  cap was not built (OneCorner : bouchon non ecrit). Where Fop holds the
  vertex without the prolonged edge (more faces at the vertex), OCCT's
  prolongation is kept.
